    <h2>What Are UTM Parameters?</h2>
    <p>UTM (Urchin Tracking Module) parameters are small tags added to the end of a URL that tell Google Analytics exactly where a visitor came from. Without them, Analytics can only guess at traffic sources using referrer data, which breaks down completely for email, social apps, and offline campaigns (QR codes, print ads) that don't pass a referrer at all.</p>

    <h2>The Five UTM Parameters Explained</h2>
    <div style="overflow-x: auto;">
      <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:16px 0; font-size:0.95rem;">
        <thead>
          <tr style="border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;">
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Parameter</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Answers</th>
            <th style="text-align:left; padding:10px 8px;">Example</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>utm_source</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Which site or platform sent the traffic</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">newsletter, facebook, google</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>utm_medium</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">What type of traffic it is</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">email, cpc, social, referral</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>utm_campaign</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Which specific campaign or promotion</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">spring_sale_2026</td></tr>
          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid rgba(128,128,128,0.3);"><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>utm_term</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Which paid keyword triggered the ad</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">running+shoes</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding:10px 8px;"><code>utm_content</code></td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">Which specific ad or link variant, for A/B tests</td><td style="padding:10px 8px;">logolink vs. textlink</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Common UTM Mistakes</h2>
    <ul class="service-points">
      <li><strong>Inconsistent capitalization or spelling</strong>, "Facebook", "facebook", and "FB" all show up as three separate sources in your reports instead of one</li>
      <li><strong>Spaces in parameter values</strong>, use underscores or hyphens instead (spaces get encoded as "%20" and become hard to read in reports)</li>
      <li><strong>Tagging internal links</strong>, never add UTM parameters to links between pages on your own site; it resets the visitor's original session source in Analytics</li>
      <li><strong>No naming convention</strong>, agree on a source/medium/campaign naming standard before a team starts tagging links, or your reports fragment into dozens of near-duplicate labels</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related Questions About UTM Tag Generators</h2>
    <h3>UTM Tag Generator: What It Builds</h3>
    <p>This <strong>UTM tag generator</strong> adds the five UTM parameters to your link, utm_source, utm_medium, utm_campaign, utm_term and utm_content, and gives you one tagged URL to copy. It runs in your browser, so nothing is uploaded or stored. Whether you call it a UTM tags generator or a UTM tags builder, it does the same job: it turns your page URL into one tagged link.</p>
    <h3>UTM Tags: What They Tell Google Analytics</h3>
    <p>UTM tags are small additions to the end of a URL that tell Google Analytics where a visitor came from. Without them, Analytics can only guess from referrer data, which breaks down for email, social apps and offline campaigns such as QR codes and print ads that pass no referrer at all.</p>
    <h3>Which Fields to Fill in a UTM Tag Maker</h3>
    <p>In this UTM generator, source, medium and campaign are the three fields Analytics needs to attribute traffic. Source is the site or platform that sent the visitor, such as newsletter or facebook, medium is the type of traffic, such as email, cpc or social, and campaign names the promotion, such as spring_sale_2026. Term and content are optional and mainly useful for paid search keywords and A/B tests of ad variations.</p>
    <h3>UTM Tag Builder: Keep Names Consistent</h3>
    <p>Capital letters and spelling matter: Facebook, facebook and FB show up as three separate sources in your reports. Use underscores or hyphens instead of spaces, which are encoded as %20, and agree a naming standard before a team starts tagging links. A custom tag builder like this one does not enforce naming, so consistency is up to you.</p>
    <h3>URL Tag Builder: Where Not to Use Tags</h3>
    <p>Use UTM links only for links coming from outside your site, such as ads, emails and social posts. Adding them to links between your own pages can overwrite the visitor's original traffic source in Analytics.</p>
    <h3>Do UTM Tagged URLs Affect SEO Rankings?</h3>
    <p>No. UTM-tagged URLs are for analytics and have no effect on search rankings. Make sure your page has a proper canonical tag, so search engines do not treat the tagged URL as a separate page from the clean one. You can check it with our <a href="/tools/canonical-tag-checker">canonical tag checker</a>.</p>
    <h2>Free UTM Builder FAQs</h2>
    <div class="faq-list" style="margin-top: 22px;">
      <details>
        <summary>Do UTM parameters affect my SEO or page ranking?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. UTM-tagged URLs are just for analytics tracking and have no effect on search rankings. Just make sure your page has a proper canonical tag so search engines don't treat the tagged URL as a separate page from the clean one.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Which fields are actually required?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Google Analytics needs source, medium, and campaign to attribute traffic properly. Term and content are optional and mainly useful for paid search and A/B testing specific ad variations.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Should I use UTM links for internal navigation?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. UTM parameters are meant for links coming from outside your site (ads, emails, social posts). Adding them to internal links can overwrite the visitor's original traffic source in Analytics.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will a long UTM URL look unprofessional in an email or ad?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>It can. Many marketers run the final tagged URL through a link shortener for anything customer-facing, while keeping the full UTM link for their own tracking records.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this tool store or send my data anywhere?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>No. The URL is built entirely in your browser with JavaScript, nothing is uploaded, logged, or stored on any server, including ours.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is there a free UTM tag generator with no signup?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. This one is free, needs no account and builds the tagged URL in your browser.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What is the difference between utm_source and utm_medium?<span class="faq-icon"></span></summary>
        <p>utm_source says which site or platform sent the traffic, such as newsletter, facebook or google. utm_medium says what type of traffic it is, such as email, cpc, social or referral.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

